CORE
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ inherited conversion: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.lookup.unqual]/5, [namespace.udecl]/1: a name in the
conversion-type-id of `using B::operator I;' is looked up in B and then by
unqualified lookup from the using-declaration, which finds the member
typedef A::I.  The front end reported "symbol 'I' is unknown" (bin53): it
looked the name up only from B's scope.  libstdc++'s
atomic<int> (and the other integral explicit specializations) declare
`using __base_type::operator __integral_type;', so std::atomic<int> was a
conversion error (cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_atomic.desc).
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program (-std=c++98), and it fails only
its WRONG assertion.
